<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tributary system</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as tributary relations</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A tributary system is an arrangement in which subordinate rulers or states acknowledge a superior power by sending regular tribute, often in exchange for recognition, protection or trade access. The best-known example is the Chinese tributary order under the Tang and Ming dynasties, which structured relations with neighbouring states. Similar systems underpinned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tributary-system.jpg" alt="Tributary system" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_procession_of_high-ranking_Assyrian_officials_followed_by_tribute_bearers_from_Urartu._From_Khorsabad,_Iraq._Iraq_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A tribute is wealth, often in kind, that one party gives to another as a sign of submission, allegiance or respect. Various ancient states exacted tribute from the rulers of lands they conquered, and in alliances lesser parties might pay tribute to more powerful ones as a sign of allegiance. Tribute differs from taxes: it is not collected in the same regular, routine manner, and it uniquely requires the payer to recognise political submission to the payee.</p><p>Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> received tribute from states including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> received tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and provinces it conquered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> used tribute to maintain control over conquered areas. Rather than replacing local rulers or colonising, they collected tribute and ideally did not interfere in local affairs unless it went unpaid. Strategic provinces, regarded as client states, paid consensually in exchange for good relations, while tributary provinces were required to pay regularly whether they wished to or not.</p><p>Aztec officials managed the system at several levels. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calpixque">calpixque</a> collected, transported and received tribute in each province, the huecalpixque managed them, and the petlacalcatl, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, oversaw the whole system. A military official, the cuahtlatoani, was involved only when newly conquered provinces resisted. Tribute took the form of natural resources such as obsidian and salt, of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and jade, and of goods and rotational labour owed by commoners to their lords, who could also call them for military service and construction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> received tribute from states under the influence of Confucian civilisation and gave them Chinese products and recognition of their authority in return. The relationship is known as Jimi, Cefeng or Chaogong. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a>, the tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had entered tributary relationships with China by the first century, though Japan later left the system during the Heian period. Goguryeo sent a diplomatic representative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in 32 CE, and China's tributary relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> continued in varying degrees until China's defeat in the Sino-Japanese War of 1894 to 1895.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> ended its suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> with the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> in 1885, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a>. The theoretical justification was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>, under which the Chinese emperor ruled with the authority of heaven. The Islamic Caliphate introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, a tax on adult men only, unlike Roman tribute, which could be levied on land, landowners and slaveholders. Iberian Christians translated it as tributo, and the Spanish empire later applied it in the New World.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tribute” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>tributary system</b> preserved more of Mesoamerica's political and cultural diversity than the Aztecs' reputation as conquerors might suggest: by governing through local rulers rather than replacing them, the empire left in place administrative structures and traditions that the Spanish colonial system could later co-opt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian economy rested on highland agriculture, Red Sea trade, and a <b>tributary system</b> through which incorporated sultanates supplied revenue to the imperial centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century Nkore turned from defence to expansion, raiding Buhweju, Igara, the Mpororo successor chiefdoms and other neighbours and drawing them into <b>tributary relations</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Kingdom of Ankole · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Obligations to the state and royal court were enforced through a <b>tributary system</b> that drew subject chiefdoms into the imperial economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Diplomatic engagement, including <b>tributary relations</b> with Ming China, helped stabilise regional trade networks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Zheng He's expeditions (1405–1433) extended China's diplomatic and commercial reach to Southeast Asia, South Asia, Arabia and East Africa, establishing <b>tributary relations</b> and displaying imperial prestige.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
